#{[17592186120339 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(H),$ we need to check if the equation $Hx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[H\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[H\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=7 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 0 &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the false statement $0=-2.$ \n\nTherefore, the system is inconsistent, which implies that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(H).$"] [17592186120397 :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ then the equation $Ax=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & 8 & 14 \\\\ -2 & -11 & -15 \\\\ 1 & 6 & k+5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 6 & k+5 \\\\ -2 & -11 & -15 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 14\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 6 & k+5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2k-5 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 14\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 6 & k+5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2k-5 \\\\ 0 & -10 & -3k-1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+10{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 6 & k+5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2k-5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 17k-51\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=k+5 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=2k-5 \\\\ 0 &=17k-51.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=17k-51,$ which means that $k=3.$"] [17592186408162 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(D),$ we need to check if the equation $Dx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[D\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[D\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 6 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 5 & -1 & 6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 5 & -1 & 6\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}-2{x}_{4} &=1 \\\\ 0 &=6 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} &=8 \\\\ {x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}-{x}_{4} &=6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the  second equation, we get a false statement $0=6.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(D).$"] [17592186408163 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(K),$ we need to check if the equation $Kx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[K\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[K\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -8 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -8 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -8 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -18\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=5 \\\\ 0 &=-18.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the false statement $0=-18.$ \n\nTherefore, the system is inconsistent, which implies that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(K).$"] [17592186408164 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ we need to check if the equation $Ax=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -4 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 10 & -7 & 11 \\\\ 3 & -12 & 10 & -13\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -4 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -12 & 10 & -13\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -4 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-4 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=3 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting it into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-(-1) & =3 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} & =2 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-4+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =-4+4(1)-3(-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Ax=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(A).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -13\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}=3\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{1}}+1\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{2}}+(-1)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=3+1+(-1)=3.$"] [17592186408165 :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ then the equation $Ax=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 7 & -2 & k+5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+40\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k+30\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=-5 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 0 &=k+30.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k+30,$ which means that $k=-30.$"] [17592186424110 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ we need to check if the equation $Ax=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 12 & 6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -12 & -6\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ -12{x}_{3} &=-6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting it into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2}+4\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =2 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+2 & =2 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3(0)-8\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =-2 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4 & =-2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Ax=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(A).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}=2\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{1}}+0\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0.$"] [17592186430000 :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ then the equation $Ax=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -20 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -14 \\\\ -1 & 1 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -20 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 4 & k-20\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -20 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k+8\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=-20 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=-14 \\\\ 0 &=k+8.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k+8,$ which means that $k=-8.$"] [17592186430001 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(C),$ we need to check if the equation $Cx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[C\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[C\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}4 & 1 & -4 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & ↔{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -4 & 9\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -4 & 9\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -16 & -11\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 & -18\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=5 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-3 \\\\ -9{x}_{3} &=-18.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting it into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2}+3(2) & =-3 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =5-2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =5-2(-3)-3(2) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Cx=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(C).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}=5\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{c}_{1}}+(-3)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{c}_{2}}+2\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{c}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=5\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 2=-30.$"] [17592186430002 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(K),$ we need to check if the equation $Kx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[K\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[K\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the false statement $0=3.$  \n\nTherefore, the system is inconsistent, which implies that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(K).$"] [17592186436526 :question/worked-solution "We write down $V$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[W\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{6} & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & {R}_{2}:=6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{W}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186436528 :question/worked-solution "We write down $Q$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[Q\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{Q}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186436530 :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186436531 :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 25\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186481356 :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ then the equation $Ax=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 21 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-5\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=12 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=7 \\\\ 0 &=k-5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k-5,$ which means that $k=5.$"] [17592186481379 :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(C),$ then the equation $Cx=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[C\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[C\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -4 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & k-4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ -4{x}_{2} &=6 \\\\ 0 &=k-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k-1,$ which means that $k=1.$"] [17592186551259 :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}] &  & {R}_{2}:=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186551266 :question/worked-solution "We write down $L$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[L\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186551278 :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186551286 :question/worked-solution "We write down $N$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[N\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{12} & 0 & 1\\end{array}] &  & {R}_{2}:=12{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 12\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{N}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186551296 :question/worked-solution "We write down $S$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[S\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 1\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{S}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186612413 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(B),$ we need to check if the equation $Bx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[B\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[B\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -5 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -21\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=-5 \\\\ 7{x}_{2} &=-21.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we get ${x}_{2}=-3.$ Substituting into the first equation gives  ${x}_{1}=4.$ \n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Bx=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(B).$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}=4\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{1}}+(-3)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=4\\cdot (-3)=-12.$"] [17592186612432 :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(U),$ then the equation $Ux=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[U\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[U\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 12 & -7 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & k-3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 0 & k-3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & k-1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k+2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ -3{x}_{2} &=9 \\\\ 0 &=k+2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k+2,$ which means that $k=-2.$"] [17592186612446 :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ then the equation $Ax=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 1 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & k-4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k+10\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=-4 \\\\ -{x}_{2} &=7 \\\\ 0 &=k+10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k+10,$ which means that $k=-10.$"] [17592186612472 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(C),$ we need to check if the equation $Cx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[C\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[C\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-2 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -10 & 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-2 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ 0 &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get the false statement $0=1.$ \n\nTherefore, the system is inconsistent, which implies that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(C).$"] [17592186612485 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(G),$ we need to check if the equation $Gx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[G\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[G\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -10 & -1 & -30\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=8 \\\\ -10{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=-30.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and we have one free variable ${x}_{3}.$ From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=3-\\frac{1}{10}{x}_{3},\n$$\n\nand from the first one, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=8-3{x}_{2}=-1+\\frac{3}{10}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=-1+\\frac{3}{10}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=3-\\frac{1}{10}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} &={x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $Gx=b$ is consistent which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(G).$ Since we are interested in writing $b$ as $b={x}_{1}{g}_{1}+{x}_{2}{g}_{2}$, that is to say, as a linear combination of the first two columns, then we need to set ${x}_{3}=0.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}=(-1)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{g}_{1}}+3\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{g}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-1)\\cdot 3=-3.$"] [17592186630938 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(G),$ we need to check if the equation $Gx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[G\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[G\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -5 & -7 & -5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-2-2{x}_{3},\n$$\n\nand from the first one, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =1-2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =1-2(-2-2{x}_{3})-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =5+{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=5+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=-2-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} &={x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $Gx=b$ is consistent which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(G).$ Since we are interested in writing $b$ as $b={x}_{1}{g}_{1}+{x}_{2}{g}_{2}$, that is to say, as a linear combination of the first two columns, then we need to set ${x}_{3}=0.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}=5\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{g}_{1}}+(-2)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{g}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=5\\cdot (-2)=-10.$"] [17592186655474 :question/worked-solution "We write down $K$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[K\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 16 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-16){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & -16 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{K}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -16 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186655532 :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -8 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+8{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}73 & 8 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186655535 :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"]}
